  {
    "id": 34464,
    "name": "herdr",
    "slug": "herdr",
    "former_names": [],
    "small_logo_thumb_url": "https://bookface-images.s3.amazonaws.com/small_logos/16f63118d9ed1edf6a055497860b53107c37b334.png",
    "website": "https://herdr.dev/",
    "all_locations": "Ankara, Ankara, Turkey",
    "long_description": "Developers don't run one coding agent anymore. They run fleets of them — for hours, sometimes days — across laptops, servers, and sandboxes. Keeping track of that work is the new bottleneck: which agent is blocked, which one finished, which terminal is it in, and why did everything die when the laptop closed.\r\n\r\nHerdr is the open-source home for that work. A background server owns the agents' terminal sessions on each machine, so the work survives disconnects, closed lids, and dropped SSH. Herdr detects each agent and turns the chaos into an attention queue — working, blocked, done, across every project — so you look only when an agent needs you. Every client is just a view: the bundled terminal UI, SSH from another machine, your phone. Close one, open another; the work never stops.\r\n\r\nSoftware can drive it too: a CLI, a socket API, and a plugin marketplace that passed 500 community plugins in its first month. The runtime is Apache-2.0 and stays open. Four months after launch: 25k GitHub stars, 340k downloads.",
    "one_liner": "building the open agent runtime",
    "team_size": 1,
    "industry": "B2B",
    "subindustry": "B2B -> Engineering, Product and Design",
    "launched_at": 1786038738,
